    "q-351911": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351911",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)={e}^{3x}$ about $x=0.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{3x} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+3x+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
